<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nguyen Kim</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1468">1468</a>–<a href="/years/1545">1545</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Nguyễn Kim</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nguyen Kim was a Vietnamese general who led the loyalists of the Later Le dynasty against the Mac dynasty, which had usurped the throne in 1527. He restored a Le emperor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanh-hoa">Thanh Hoa</a> in 1533. After his death in 1545 his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinh-kiem">Trinh Kiem</a> took command, while his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-hoang">Nguyen Hoang</a> founded the line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyen lords</a> in the south.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nguyen-kim.jpg" alt="Nguyen Kim" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kiennguyen546193 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tri%E1%BB%87u_T%E1%BB%95_Mi%E1%BA%BFu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nguyễn Kim claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-trai">Nguyễn Trãi</a>, one of the chief aides of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-loi">Lê Lợi</a>. His father, Nguyễn Hoang Du, was one of the leaders of the first revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mac-dang-dung">Mạc Đăng Dung</a>, a high-ranking officer of the weakened court; when that revolt was crushed his father was executed. In 1527 Mạc Đăng Dung deposed the reigning Lê monarch, Lê Cung Hoàng, and made himself ruler of Đại Việt, and a second revolt followed, led by Nguyễn Kim and his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinh-kiem">Trịnh Kiểm</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1529 Nguyễn Kim went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and submitted to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/photisarath">Photisarath</a>, who gave him the territory of Xam Neua to administer. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mac-dang-dung">Mạc Đăng Dung</a> put down the Lê loyalists in Thanh Hóa, the remnants of the old court took refuge in Nguyễn Kim's domain. In 1533 he proclaimed Prince Lê Ninh, a son of Emperor Lê Chiêu Tông, king of Đại Việt, a claim Photisarath recognised and supported with resources. Envoys went to Ming China in 1536 and 1537 to denounce Mạc Đăng Dung as a usurper and ask for help, but the Ming called off their intervention.</p><p>The war against the Mạc went on through most of the sixteenth century, the Lê slowly regaining power with the help of two military clans of Thanh Hóa, the Nguyễn and the Trịnh. Although the families were allies and linked by marriage, a rivalry grew between Nguyễn Kim and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinh-kiem">Trịnh Kiểm</a>, whose own ambitions the alliance did not restrain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Nguyễn Kim was murdered in 1545 by a Mạc general who had come to surrender, and the Trịnh gained the upper hand. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinh-kiem">Trịnh Kiểm</a> then had Nguyễn Kim's elder son killed. The youngest son, Nguyễn Hoàng, fearing the same fate, asked through his sister, Trịnh Kiểm's wife, to be made governor of the distant southern frontier lands of Thuận Hoá and Quảng Nam. Trịnh Kiểm agreed, and in 1558 Nguyễn Hoàng went south, the beginning of a political division of the country between the Trịnh and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyễn lords</a> that lasted 250 years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nguyễn Kim” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Loyalists led by <b>Nguyễn Kim</b> enthroned a Lê prince in exile in 1533, and after Nguyễn Kim's death in 1545 his son-in-law Trịnh Kiểm took control of the restored court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-le-dynasty">Later Le dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Their opponents, led by <b>Nguyen Kim</b> and then Trinh Kiem, restored a Le emperor in 1533 in Thanh Hoa, beginning the war between the Northern Mac and Southern Le courts.</blockquote><a href="/state/mac-dynasty">Mac dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1558 Nguyen Hoang, son of the Le loyalist leader <b>Nguyen Kim</b>, had himself appointed governor of Thuan Hoa in central Vietnam to escape the Trinh lords.</blockquote><a href="/state/nguyen-lords">Nguyen lords</a></li><li><blockquote>Trinh Kiem, son-in-law of the Le loyalist leader <b>Nguyen Kim</b>, took command of the Le restoration forces after Nguyen Kim's death in 1545 and became the real power behind the Later Le dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/trinh-lords">Trinh lords</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Thanh Hóa, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.81,105.78&amp;year=1518" aria-label="Thanh Hóa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7405 1430 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nguyen Kim"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7855 1700h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7855 1700h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.81,105.78&amp;year=1518">What was here in 1518? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
